ght on d5.\n- But c7-c5 DOES attack d4, not d5. d5 is one square above d4. So c5 attacks d4? No — pawn on c5 attacks d4 (diagonally forward), yes. But the knight is on d5 — that's one square further up. So c5 does **not** attack d5 either.\n\nWait — coordinates:\n\nWhite knight is at d5 — that’s file d, rank 5.\n\nBlack pawns on 7th rank: moving to 6th or 5th.\n\nA black pawn on c7 moving to c5 is now on c5 — same rank as the white knight on d5. Diagonal capture would be to d4 or b4 — both behind. So **neither c7-c5 nor e7-e5 attacks the knight on d5**.\n\nSo those moves don't gain tempo.\n\nBut what if we consider development?\n\nThe knight on b8:  \n- N: b8-a6 — develops to a6, eyeing b4 or c5 later, supports possible ...b7-b5 push.  \n- N: b8-c6 — classical development, controls d4, e5, b4, a5 — strong central influence, defends d4, prepares ...d7-d5 later.\n\nN: b8-c6 is more active than a6 — it fights for the center immediately.\n\nAlso, the white knight on d5 may become strong, so Black needs counterplay.\n\nAnother idea: prepare ...d7-d5 to strike back in the center. To do that, supporting with ...Nc6 first makes sense.\n\nR: h8-g8 is passive right now — no immediate threat on the h-file.\n\nPawn moves like a7-a5 or h7-h5 are flank moves without immediate impact.\n\nBest move is likely **N: b8-c6**, developing a piece with central control, preparing to support ...d7-d5, and increasing coordination.\n\nReason:  \nN: b8-c6 is the most principled developing move, bringing a knight into the game with central influence, supporting future ...d7-d5 break, and improving king-side connectivity. It doesn’t waste time and avoids passivity. Other moves like pawn pushes don’t contribute as much to immediate development or central equality.\n\nAnswer: N: b8-c6"}
