----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, R: a8-b8, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, N: c6-a5, N: c6-b4, N: c6-b8, N: c6-d4, N: c6-e5, P: d7-d6, P: e7-e5, P: e7-e6, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6, R: h8-g8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Evaluation:  \nLooking at the current position, Black has a significant advantage due to the advanced development and central control. The knight on c6 is well-placed and has several active moves. One of the most promising options is **N: c6-e5**, which attacks the white bishop on f4 (currently on g5 in encoded form as BWF1 — but according to the board, it\'s on f4), increases central pressure, and prepares for potential tactical ideas involving the center or kingside. However, **N: c6-d4** is also strong, jumping into the center, threatening forks (e.g., on e2 or f3), and applying immediate pressure on key squares.\n\nAnother candidate is **P: e7-e5**, aiming to challenge White’s central pawn on d4 (but there is no white pawn on d4 — actually, White has a pawn on d4? Wait: White pawn on d4 is missing — looking at the board, White has PWF1 on d4? No — row 4 shows: d4 has PWF1? Yes! There is a White pawn on d4 (PWF1). So yes, d4 is occupied by a white pawn.\n\nWait: On d4 — the board shows:  \nRow 4: `|    |    |    |PWF1|    |BWF1|    |    |` → so d4 = PWF1 (White pawn), f4 = BWF1 (White bishop).\n\nSo Black knight on c6 can go to d4, attacking the bishop on f4 and forking the pawn on d4 and potentially targeting the weakened light squares around White’s king.\n\nBut wait — **N: c6-d4** lands on d4, which is currently occupied by a white pawn (PWF1). That square is not empty. So **N: c6-d4** is invalid unless it\'s a capture — but the move list includes "N: c6-d4" as a valid move, which implies it must be a capture. But d4 has a white pawn — so if Black knight captures on d4, that would remove the white pawn.\n\nBut is that square defended? The white pawn on d4 is supported by... no other white pawn (no c-pawn nearby — c3/c2 are empty). The only possible defender is the queen on d1 or king, but it\'s not directly protected by a pawn. Let\'s check:  \nWhite pawns: a2, b2, c2? No — c2 is empty. d2 is empty. So d4 pawn is isolated and likely undefended by another pawn.\n\nBut is it defended by a piece? The white queen is on d1, so she controls the d-file — so yes, the queen defends d4. So if Black plays Nxd4, White can recapture with Qxd4. That would trade knight for pawn — not favorable.\n\nAlternatively, **N: c6-b4** goes to b4, eyeing c2 or a2, possibly forking or pressuring the queenside. But less dynamic.\n\n**N: c6-e5** jumps to e5 — this is a strong outpost, central, and attacks f3 (if knight were there) or supports f7-f5 push. But more importantly, it pressures the center and supports future ...f5 or ...Ng4 ideas. Also, e5 is not currently under attack by white pawns — white has no pawns on d4 or f4 that could recapture immediately. d4 pawn could take back if Black later plays ...f5, but not directly.\n\nWait — e5 is attacked by White’s bishop on f4? Yes — BWF1 is on f4, so it controls e5. So if Black plays Nc6-e5, White can immediately play Bxf7+ or Bxe5? Bishop on f4 attacks e5 — so **N: c6-e5** would be captured by the bishop on f4 unless protected.\n\nBut after Nxe5, Bxe5, Black loses knight for bishop — again, even trade, but Black gives up initiative.\n\nBetter idea: **P: e7-e5** — this attacks the d4 pawn immediately. If White doesn\'t respond, Black can follow up with ...exd4, winning material since the d4 pawn is only defended by the queen. After ...e5, if White plays dxe5, then dxe5, Black can play Nxe5 — now the knight is on a strong central square, and White has to deal with threats.\n\nBut ...e5 weakens d5 and f5, but in this position, it seems acceptable.\n\nAnother option: **P: f7-f5** — this is aggressive, preparing ...f4 or ...Ng4, but may weaken the king\'s position.\n\nBut better yet: **N: c6-a5** — retreats the knight, but why? Maybe to reroute via b7-c5, but slow.\n\n**R: a8-b8** — prepares to double rooks or pressure the b-file, but not urgent.\n\nBest move: **N: c6-b4** — this is interesting because b4 is not attacked by any pawn (white has no c-pawn), so the knight cannot be driven away easily. From b4, it threatens to jump to c2 (forking king and rook) or a2, or support a queenside initiative. Also, it puts pressure on the c2 square, and if White ever plays c3, then Nb4xc2 wins material.\n\nMoreover, the knight on b4 is immune from pawn attacks, and only pieces can remove it — and it\'s hard to dislodge.\n\nBut let\'s check if c2 is defended: c2 has no white pawn — White’s pawns on a2, b2, but c2 is empty. The king is on e1, queen on d1 — so c2 is defended by the king? No — c2 is on the same diagonal as the black bishop on c8? Not yet. But White’s king on e1 — does it control c2? No. Queen on d1 controls c2? Yes — along the rank. So Qd1 protects c2.\n\nSo Nb4-c2 would be met with Qd1xc2 — again, trading knight for queen? No — knight takes on c2, queen takes back — so knight for queen is bad.\n\nBut if White is forced to recapture with queen, it may disrupt her development.\n\nStill, not immediately winning.\n\nBack to **P: e7-e5** — this is strong because:\n- Attacks the d4 pawn, which is only defended by the queen.\n- If White ignores it, Black plays ...exd4, and if Qxd4, then Nxe5 — knight enters powerfully.\n- It opens diagonals for the queen and bishop.\n- It gains space and initiative.\n\nAlso, e5 prevents White from expanding with e4.\n\nAfter **P: e7-e5**, White must respond to the threat to d4.\n\nThus, **P: e7-e5** is a central break that challenges White’s center and creates immediate tactical tension.\n\nReason:  \nPlaying **P: e7-e5** strikes at the base of White’s center, targets the isolated d4 pawn defended only by the queen, and opens lines for Black’s pieces. It’s a dynamic move that seizes the initiative and forces White into a reactive position. Alternatives like knight moves are playable but less impactful in the short term.\n\nAnswer: P: e7-e5'}
