  * `P` - Pawn\n        * Color (Player):\n            * `W` - White (moves first)\n            * `B` - Black\n        * 2Step (En Passant flag)\n            * `F` - Did NOT move 2 squares last turn\n            * `T` - Moved 2 squares last turn (en passant eligible)\n        * Moved (Movement flag)\n            * `1` - Has moved before\n            * `0` - Has NOT moved yet\n        \n        ### Examples: \n            * PWF1: White Pawn, not available for en passant, already moved; \n            * RBF0: Black Rook, never moved (castling eligible);\n            * KWT1: White king, irrelevant 2-step flag, has moved;\n            * QWF0 = White queen, irrelevant 2-step flag, never moved.\n\n        Now is your turn, to your best ability, choose your move to maximize your chances of winning.\n        Please briefly evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output one of the valid actions with specified format.\n        Your response format should be:\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\n        ', 'user_prompt': 'You are playing as Black.\nCurrent state representation is as follows:\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n8 |RBF0|    |    |    |KBF0|BBF0|    |RBF0| 8\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n7 |PBF0|PBF0|BWF1|BBF1|    |    |PBF0|PBF0| 7\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n6 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 6\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n5 |    |    |    |PBF1|QWF1|PBT1|    |    | 5\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n4 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 4\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n3 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 3\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n2 |PWF0|PWF0|    |    |PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0| 2\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n1 |RWF0|    |    |    |KWF0|BWF0|NWF0|RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[B: d7-e6, K: e8-f7, B: f8-e7]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Evaluation:  \n- **B: d7-e6**: This move develops the light-squared bishop to a strong diagonal, threatening the white queen on e5 (if it were still there) and supporting potential central control. However, in this position, it retreats slightly and may not exert immediate pressure. It does prepare for kingside castling or future central play, but doesn’t exploit any tactical weaknesses.\n\n- **K: e8-f7**: Moving the king out of safety so early is highly risky. While it connects rooks indirectly, it forfeits castling rights and walks into an open file where White can easily launch attacks with pieces. This move weakens Black’s king position significantly and should be avoided unless absolutely necessary — which it isn’t here.\n\n- **B: f8-e7**: This move brings the dark-squared bishop toward the center, unblocking the king’s side and preparing for potential castling. It also keeps options open for future maneuvering or defense, especially since the king is still in the corner. Compared to d7-e6, this development is more natural and safer, as it doesn’t block potential pawn breaks like ...e5.\n\nReason:  \nB: f8-e7 is the most principled move among the choices — it contributes to piece development, helps prepare kingside castling, and maintains king safety. Unlike moving the king prematurely (K: e8-f7), or making a passive bishop retreat (B: d7-e6), this move actively improves piece coordination without risk.\n\nAnswer: B: f8-e7'}
